INT. TRAIN DINING CAR - EVENING

A vintage dining car on a night run through the Alps. White linen, silver cutlery, brass lamps swaying gently. Pine forests slide past in the dark windows.

At a corner table, three people share a quiet dinner: INSPECTOR AUGUSTIN BELLWETHER (50s, rumpled tweed, small notebook peeking from a pocket), COUNTESS ISOLDE VON RAMSAUER (60s, pearls, a lorgnette), and a young woman, PRISCA ABERNETHY-LUND (20s, secretary, gloves in her lap).

The train sways.

BELLWETHER
You were saying, Countess?

ISOLDE
I was saying that I did not like the man.

BELLWETHER
So you said.

ISOLDE
I said it twice. I'll say it a third time.

PRISCA
Countess, please.

ISOLDE
Do not shush me, child. He is dead. I am entitled to my opinion.

Bellwether sets down his fork. The lamp swings. He and Prisca both reach for the salt at once, collide, and stop. She withdraws her hand with a nervous laugh. He takes the salt and sprinkles it.

BELLWETHER
Miss Abernethy-Lund, you worked for Mr. Vanterpool.

PRISCA
Three years.

BELLWETHER
And you saw him last at--

PRISCA
Seven. In his compartment. He asked me for a pen.

BELLWETHER
And you gave him one.

PRISCA
Yes.

BELLWETHER
A green one.

Prisca stares at her gloves.

PRISCA
Yes.

ISOLDE
What of it?

BELLWETHER
There was no pen in the compartment, Countess.

The train enters a tunnel. The windows go black. The lamps flicker. When they return, the three are in exactly the same positions, though no one is eating.

BELLWETHER (CONT'D)
(mildly)
It is a small thing. But small things are my profession.

ISOLDE
Inspector, are you accusing my companion?

BELLWETHER
I'm observing. And I would like a quiet dinner. So, if you please, we shall continue after dessert.

He picks up his fork again. After a moment, Isolde does the same. Prisca, pale, follows.
